DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       These eleven functions were defined in C99, and describe  the  handling
       of  floating‐point  rounding  and  exceptions  (overflow,  zero‐divide,
       etc.).

   EExxcceeppttiioonnss
       The _d_i_v_i_d_e_‐_b_y_‐_z_e_r_o exception occurs when an operation on finite numbers
       produces infinity as exact answer.

       The _o_v_e_r_f_l_o_w exception occurs when a result has to be represented as  a
       floating‐point  number,  but  has (much) larger absolute value than the
       largest (finite) floating‐point number that is representable.

       The _u_n_d_e_r_f_l_o_w exception occurs when a result has to be represented as a
       floating‐point number, but has smaller absolute value than the smallest
       positive normalized floating‐point number (and would lose much accuracy
       when represented as a denormalized number).

       The _i_n_e_x_a_c_t exception occurs when the rounded result of an operation is
       not equal to the infinite precision  result.   It  may  occur  whenever
       _o_v_e_r_f_l_o_w or _u_n_d_e_r_f_l_o_w occurs.

       The  _i_n_v_a_l_i_d  exception occurs when there is no well‐defined result for
       an operation, as for 0/0 or infinity - infinity or sqrt(-1).

   EExxcceeppttiioonn hhaannddlliinngg
       Exceptions are represented in two ways:  as  a  single  bit  (exception
       present/absent),  and  these bits correspond in some implementation‐de‐
       fined way with bit positions in an  integer,  and  also  as  an  opaque
       structure  that  may contain more information about the exception (per‐
       haps the code address where it occurred).

       Each of the macros FFEE__DDIIVVBBYYZZEERROO, FFEE__IINNEEXXAACCTT,  FFEE__IINNVVAALLIIDD,  FFEE__OOVVEERRFFLLOOWW,
       FFEE__UUNNDDEERRFFLLOOWW  is  defined  when the implementation supports handling of
       the corresponding exception, and if so then defines  the  corresponding
       bit(s), so that one can call exception handling functions, for example,
       using  the integer argument FFEE__OOVVEERRFFLLOOWW|FFEE__UUNNDDEERRFFLLOOWW.  Other exceptions
       may be supported.  The macro FFEE__AALLLL__EEXXCCEEPPTT is the  bitwise  OR  of  all
       bits corresponding to supported exceptions.

       The  ffeecclleeaarreexxcceepptt()  function  clears  the supported exceptions repre‐
       sented by the bits in its argument.

       The ffeeggeetteexxcceeppttffllaagg() function stores a representation of the state  of
       the  exception  flags represented by the argument _e_x_c_e_p_t_s in the opaque
       object _*_f_l_a_g_p.

       The ffeerraaiisseeeexxcceepptt() function raises  the  supported  exceptions  repre‐
       sented by the bits in _e_x_c_e_p_t_s.

       The  ffeesseetteexxcceeppttffllaagg() function sets the complete status for the excep‐
       tions represented by _e_x_c_e_p_t_s to the value _*_f_l_a_g_p.  This value must have
       been obtained by an earlier call of ffeeggeetteexxcceeppttffllaagg() with a last argu‐
       ment that contained all bits in _e_x_c_e_p_t_s.

       The ffeetteesstteexxcceepptt() function returns a word in which the  bits  are  set
       that  were  set in the argument _e_x_c_e_p_t_s and for which the corresponding
       exception is currently set.

   RRoouunnddiinngg mmooddee
       The rounding mode determines how the result  of  floating‐point  opera‐
       tions  is  treated when the result cannot be exactly represented in the
       significand.  Various rounding modes may be provided: round to  nearest
       (the  default), round up (toward positive infinity), round down (toward
       negative infinity), and round toward zero.

       Each of the macros FFEE__TTOONNEEAARREESSTT,  FFEE__UUPPWWAARRDD,  FFEE__DDOOWWNNWWAARRDD,  and  FFEE__TTOO‐‐
       WWAARRDDZZEERROO  is  defined when the implementation supports getting and set‐
       ting the corresponding rounding direction.

       The ffeeggeettrroouunndd() function returns the macro corresponding to  the  cur‐
       rent rounding mode.

       The  ffeesseettrroouunndd()  function  sets the rounding mode as specified by its
       argument and returns zero when it was successful.

       C99 and POSIX.1‐2008 specify  an  identifier,  FFLLTT__RROOUUNNDDSS,  defined  in
       _<_f_l_o_a_t_._h_>, which indicates the implementation‐defined rounding behavior
       for  floating‐point addition.  This identifier has one of the following
       values:

       --11     The rounding mode is not determinable.

       00      Rounding is toward 0.

       11      Rounding is toward nearest number.

       22      Rounding is toward positive infinity.

       33      Rounding is toward negative infinity.

       Other values represent machine‐dependent, nonstandard rounding modes.

       The value of FFLLTT__RROOUUNNDDSS should reflect the current rounding mode as set
       by ffeesseettrroouunndd() (but see BUGS).

   FFllooaattiinngg‐‐ppooiinntt eennvviirroonnmmeenntt
       The entire floating‐point environment, including control modes and sta‐
       tus flags, can be handled as one opaque object, of  type  _f_e_n_v___t.   The
       default  environment is denoted by FFEE__DDFFLL__EENNVV (of type _c_o_n_s_t _f_e_n_v___t _*).
       This is the environment setup at program start and it is defined by ISO
       C to have round to nearest, all exceptions cleared and a nonstop  (con‐
       tinue on exceptions) mode.

       The ffeeggeetteennvv() function saves the current floating‐point environment in
       the object _*_e_n_v_p.

       The  ffeehhoollddeexxcceepptt()  function  does the same, then clears all exception
       flags, and sets a nonstop (continue on exceptions) mode, if  available.
       It returns zero when successful.

       The  ffeesseetteennvv()  function  restores the floating‐point environment from
       the object _*_e_n_v_p.  This object must be known to be valid, for  example,
       the  result  of  a  call  to  ffeeggeetteennvv()  or ffeehhoollddeexxcceepptt() or equal to
       FFEE__DDFFLL__EENNVV.  This call does not raise exceptions.

       The ffeeuuppddaatteeeennvv() function installs the floating‐point environment rep‐
       resented by the object _*_e_n_v_p, except that currently  raised  exceptions
       are  not  cleared.   After calling this function, the raised exceptions
       will be a bitwise OR of those previously set with those in  _*_e_n_v_p.   As
       before, the object _*_e_n_v_p must be known to be valid.

