       If possible, the GNU C Library defines a macro FFEE__NNOOMMAASSKK__EENNVV which rep‐
       resents an environment where every exception raised causes a trap to
       occur.  You can test for this macro using ##iiffddeeff.  It is defined only
       if __GGNNUU__SSOOUURRCCEE is defined.  The C99 standard does not define a way to
       set individual bits in the floating‐point mask, for example, to trap on
       specific flags.  Since glibc 2.2, glibc supports the functions ffeeeenn‐‐
       aabblleeeexxcceepptt() and ffeeddiissaabblleeeexxcceepptt() to set individual floating‐point
       traps, and ffeeggeetteexxcceepptt() to query the state.

       ##ddeeffiinnee __GGNNUU__SSOOUURRCCEE         /* See feature_test_macros(7) */
       ##iinncclluuddee <<ffeennvv..hh>>

       iinntt ffeeeennaabblleeeexxcceepptt((iinntt _e_x_c_e_p_t_s));;
       iinntt ffeeddiissaabblleeeexxcceepptt((iinntt _e_x_c_e_p_t_s));;
       iinntt ffeeggeetteexxcceepptt((vvooiidd));;

       The ffeeeennaabblleeeexxcceepptt() and ffeeddiissaabblleeeexxcceepptt() functions enable (disable)
       traps for each of the exceptions represented by _e_x_c_e_p_t_s and return the
       previous set of enabled exceptions when successful, and -1 otherwise.
       The ffeeggeetteexxcceepptt() function returns the set of all currently enabled ex‐
       ceptions.

